Q: Different UICollectionViews in UITableView objective-C A: <p>Any implementation of a table view or a collection view should have a dedicated model. </p>

<p>Simply pass the model down.</p>

<p>NSArray > NSArray > Profile Pictures</p>

<pre><code>- (UITableViewCell *)tableView:(UITableView *)tableView cellForRowAtIndexPath:(NSIndexPath *)indexPath {
    CustomCell *cell = [tableView dequeueReusableCellWithIdentifier:@"Cell"];
    NSArray *list = self.lists[indexPath.row];
    [cell configureWithList:list];
    return cell;
</code></pre>

<p>}</p>

<pre><code>@interface CustomTableViewCell () &lt;UICollectionViewDataSource&gt;
@property (nonatomic, weak) NSArray *list;
@property (nonatomic, weak) IBOutlet UICollectionView *collectionView;
@end

@implementation CustomTableViewCell

-(void)setList:(NSArray *)list {
    _list = list;
    [self.collectionView reloadData];
}

-(void)configureWithList:(NSArray *)list {
    self.list = list;
}
</code></pre>

<p>EDIT: After reading this, I decided to build this control -> <a href="https://www.cocoacontrols.com/controls/rrncollectiontableviewsections" rel="nofollow">cocoacontrols</a></p>

Q: Typing the Enter/Return key in Selenium A: <p>There are the following ways of pressing keys - C#:</p>

<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Driver.FindElement(By.Id(&quot;Value&quot;)).SendKeys(Keys.Return);
</code></pre>
<p>OR</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>OpenQA.Selenium.Interactions.Actions action = new OpenQA.Selenium.Interactions.Actions(Driver);
action.SendKeys(OpenQA.Selenium.Keys.Escape);
</code></pre>
<p>OR</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>IWebElement body = GlobalDriver.FindElement(By.TagName(&quot;body&quot;));
body.SendKeys(Keys.Escape);
</code></pre>

Q: How to convert bool [] into byte [] A: <p>To be performant you'll need to operate on multiple bytes at once instead of individual bools. It can be done by casting with a safe <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.runtime.interopservices.memorymarshal.cast?view=net-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>MemoryMarshal.Cast</code></a> or an <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.runtime.compilerservices.unsafe?view=net-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Unsafe</code></a> load. Here's the best scalar solution on platforms with a parallel bit extract instruction like <a href="https://www.felixcloutier.com/x86/pext" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PEXT on x86</a>, <a href="https://github.com/riscv/riscv-bitmanip" rel="nofollow noreferrer">BEXT on RISC-V</a>, or <a href="https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0602/2022-06/SVE-Instructions/BEXT--Gather-lower-bits-from-positions-selected-by-bitmask-" rel="nofollow noreferrer">BEXT on ARM</a></p>
<pre><code>using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

bool[] b6 = new bool[] {true, true, true, true, true, false, true, true,
                        true, false, true, false, true, true, false, false };

// Cast the input array into uint64[]
Span&lt;ulong&gt; ulongs = MemoryMarshal.Cast&lt;byte, ulong&gt;(b6);

const ulong mask = 0x0101010101010101UL;

// Extract the 8 low bits in each ulong and pack into `out[]` bytes
byte data[] = new byte[2];
data[0] = (byte)Bmi2.X64.ParallelBitExtract(ulongs[0], mask);
data[1] = (byte)Bmi2.X64.ParallelBitExtract(ulongs[1], mask);
</code></pre>
<p>But if you don't have a fast PEXT instruction then there's still a very fast generic scalar solution like this</p>
<pre><code>byte PackBytesLE(ulong b)
{
    return byte(0x8040201008040201 * b &gt;&gt; 56);
}

byte[] data = new byte[2];
// Extract the low bits from the ulong[] array above
data[0] = PackBytesLE(ulongs[0]);
data[1] = PackBytesLE(ulongs[1]);
</code></pre>
<p>On a big-endian machine you can use this instead of <code>PackBytesLE</code> for better performance</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>byte PackBytesBE(long b)
{
    return byte(0x0102040810204080 * b &gt;&gt; 56);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This uses the multiplication technique that you can find more explanation in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/51750902/995714">this question</a></p>
<hr />
<p>If you want to convert a lot of bytes then a SIMD version would be the best. With SSE2 you can extract 2 bytes at once like in your example input array. Here's an AVX2 solution which can extract 32 bits from 32 bytes at a time</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>// Cast the bool array to Vector256 span
ReadOnlySpan&lt;Vector256&lt;byte&gt;&gt; vectors = MemoryMarshal.Cast&lt;byte, Vector256&lt;byte&gt;&gt;(bytes);
Vector256&lt;byte&gt; v = ReverseVectorByteOrder(vectors[0]);

// Or just read directly
Vector256&lt;byte&gt; v =
    Unsafe.ReadUnaligned&lt;Vector256&lt;byte&gt;&gt;(
        ref MemoryMarshal.GetReference(bytes));
v = ReverseVectorByteOrder(v);

// Move LSB -&gt; MSB to extract using pmovmskb
v = Avx2.ShiftLeftLogical(v.AsUInt16(), 7).AsByte();

uint m = (uint)Avx2.MoveMask(v); // Packed 32-bit result
byte[] outBytes = new byte[4];   // Get them into individual bytes if you want
m = BinaryPrimitives.ReverseEndianness(m);
MemoryMarshal.Write(outBytes, in m);
</code></pre>
<p>Unsafe store can also be used if available</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>unsafe
{
    fixed (byte* p = outBytes)
        *(int*)p = m;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you have AVX-512 then it's even better, you'll be able to get 64 bits at once. But since this uses platform-specific intrinsics you'll need to adapt for other architectures if necessary. Obviously there's also <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.numerics.vector-1?view=net-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>System.Numerics.Vector</code></a>, you can try it if you want a cross-platform vectorized solution</p>
<p>See <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/zxBeQv" rel="nofollow noreferrer">demo on dotnetfiddle for all the above solutions</a></p>

Q: C: How to assign for loop characters = for loop values without printing(For equation purposes) A: <p>In evaluating a series of digits as in your program, this looks to be a preliminary stab at totaling up the digit values for a Luhn check.  The progression of digits is correct; however, there is a bit more nuance in deriving a total for performing the Luhn check digit check, which you might have already surmised.  Still, just to take your code to the next phase where a quick check of the entered series of digits would pass the check digit test, following is a refactored version of the program.</p>
<pre><code>#include &quot;cs50.h&quot;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;

int main(void)
{
    bool odd = true;
    int sum = 0, take_credit, work;

    long credit = get_long(&quot;What's your Credit Number?(No Hyphens) &quot;);

    for (char letter= 'a'; credit != credit / 10; letter++)
    {
        take_credit = credit % 10;

        if (odd)
        {
            sum += take_credit;         /* Odd number digits are just added to the summary test total   */
            odd = false;
        }
        else
        {
            work = take_credit * 2;     /* Even number digits are multiplied by &quot;2&quot;, then tested        */

            if (work &gt; 9)               /* If value is greater than &quot;9&quot; subtract &quot;9&quot; from the value     */
                work -= 9;              /* This is effectively the same as adding the digits            */

            sum += work;
            odd = true;
        }

        printf(&quot;%c = %d  Running summary: %d\n&quot;, letter, take_credit, sum);

        credit= credit / 10;
    }

    if ((sum % 10) == 0)                /* If sum total is divisible by &quot;10&quot;, it would pass the test    */
        printf(&quot;Sum %d would pass Luhn test\n&quot;, sum);
    else
        printf(&quot;Sum %d would not pass Luhn test\n&quot;, sum);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>In reviewing other issues regarding credit card number checks, this is one of the usual methods for parsing an inputted credit card number, and then applying the rules of adding odd and even numbered digit values to a working sum, and then checking the sum to see if it is divisible by &quot;10&quot;.  As your program has done, it is effectively parsing the number from right to left.  The additional refactoring is just starting the summation test with the rightmost digit being an odd numbered digit and then alternating digit testing between even and odd numbered digits.</p>
<p>Following is a couple of tests with the terminal output.  The first test utilizes your test value, and the second test utilizes a theoretical credit card number of sixteen digits.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/cc_3/bin/Release$ ./cc_3 
What's your Credit Number?(No Hyphens) 1400214
a = 4  Running summary: 4
b = 1  Running summary: 6
c = 2  Running summary: 8
d = 0  Running summary: 8
e = 0  Running summary: 8
f = 4  Running summary: 16
g = 1  Running summary: 17
Sum 17 would not pass Luhn test
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/cc_3/bin/Release$ ./cc_3 
What's your Credit Number?(No Hyphens) 5555555555554444
a = 4  Running summary: 4
b = 4  Running summary: 12
c = 4  Running summary: 16
d = 4  Running summary: 24
e = 5  Running summary: 29
f = 5  Running summary: 30
g = 5  Running summary: 35
h = 5  Running summary: 36
i = 5  Running summary: 41
j = 5  Running summary: 42
k = 5  Running summary: 47
l = 5  Running summary: 48
m = 5  Running summary: 53
n = 5  Running summary: 54
o = 5  Running summary: 59
p = 5  Running summary: 60
Sum 60 would pass Luhn test
</code></pre>
<p>This refactored code is an iterative step towards a program that would validate credit card numbers, as there probably would need to be additional tests for proper length, valid starting digits to identify the credit card vendor, and so forth.  However, the coding is on the correct path.</p>
<p>The takeaway here mostly is delving further into checking algorithms such as the Luhn algorithm.</p>

Q: &quot;z&quot; is a variable but used like type A: <p>The comments have already rightfully questioned why this approach is even there. I'll focus this answer on getting the job done without solving the compiler error in itself.</p>
<p>As <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/884561/kenneth-k">@kenneth-k</a> has already said, you have to use a type expression inside <code>&lt;&gt;</code> to make it work. Since you are using reflection to refer to that target type, you have to completely forget about static typing and describe everything in your reflection logic.</p>
<p>First, getting a type with <code>Type.GetType(className, assemblyName)</code> already seems fishy. Is it an internal type that we don't have direct access to? Do we really need that type? Should/could we improve the accessibility constraints to avoid typing it with reflection?</p>
<p>Besides the above, the main logic is written in a very verbose manner, with too many and insignificant casts. <code>propertyInfo.SetValue</code> for example does not need you to cast your objects, no matter what they are, because we're already in reflection territory. The best reason why you would do that is to give type semantics to your code for sanity check, and assert that your assumptions are correct. In this case, <code>((List&lt;T&gt;)output.Response)[0]</code> that is repeated 3 times (once as <code>List&lt;z&gt;</code> and twice as <code>List&lt;Policy&gt;</code> which also a possibly incorrect logic), could be stored in a variable and asserted in what kind of type it is.</p>
<p>Then, <code>value</code> is only used beside a cast like this: <code>((List&lt;Document&gt;)value)</code>. You could have asserted that the value is indeed <code>List&lt;Document&gt;</code>, and then stored that in a <code>List&lt;Document&gt;</code> variable so you avoid the multiple casts around.</p>
<p>Ultimately, the logic you're trying to apply in the inner <code>foreach</code> is finding a property with the following constraints:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>p =&gt; p.CanRead &amp;&amp; p.GetMethod.IsPublic</code></li>
<li><code>p =&gt; p.Name == ((List&lt;Document&gt;)value)[0].GetType().Name</code></li>
</ul>
<p>The above code can be simplified to the following (omitting assertions):</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var outputResponse = ((System.Collections.IEnumerable)output.Response).Cast&lt;object&gt;();

foreach (var key in abc.Keys)
{
    var value = abc[key];
    var documentListValue = value as List&lt;Document&gt;;
    var firstDocument = documentListValue[0];
    var elementType = outputResponse.First().GetType();
    var elementProperties = elementType.GetProperties()
        .Where(p =&gt; p.CanRead &amp;&amp; p.GetMethod.IsPublic)
        .Where(p =&gt; p.Name == firstDocument.GetType().Name);

    foreach (var property in elementProperties)
    {
        property.SetValue(outputResponse, documentListValue);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The above avoids caring about the actual type of the contents inside the <code>output.Response</code> object assuming it's an enumerable of any element type (<code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> implements the <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> and therefore also the non-generic <code>IEnumerable</code> interface). Also, by constraining the element's properties further (notice the second <code>Where</code>), we avoid extra checks and extra checks on the properties themselves. That is assuming that <code>abc</code> is completely irrelevant to the inner logic. If it plays a role in the filtered properties, the above simplification is incorrect, although the principles still apply.</p>
<p>The code snippet above focuses on trying to filter down the properties whose values to set, based on the constraints that were given. Despite using reflection, the key is that we do not exactly care about the actual type of the objects being written (<code>outputResponse</code>), as long as they have the properties we are writing via reflection. <code>elementProperties</code> has been filtered based on the properties <code>outputResponse</code> contains, and thus it should never fail.</p>
<hr />
<p>Note that in every case I mention asserting, I mean using either manually throwing with some conditions, or using <code>Debug.Assert</code>, <code>Contract.Xyz</code> methods, depending on your use case. For example:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var documentListValue = value as List&lt;Document&gt;;
if (documentListValue == null)
{
    throw new ArgumentException(&quot;The value was not a list of documents&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>By applying assertions like the above you make your reflection code easier to understand, maintain and reason about. When you drift away from static typing, it's too easy to break things when unexpected values are received, and such assertions can at least mitigate parts of that risk.</p>
